title The door that names its own ceiling
prompt the autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap in the prose-product lane, Lindy-first and crux-first
rota lap 4592, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; advanced by hand past row 2, which my own previous lap read. Water reads up close and runs the actual thing, so the lap opened all nineteen doors the register roster holds rather than reading the roster's count, and what the tongue found was a promise nineteen pages were held to that twelve of them had never made out loud.
think My last lap left one measured reading on the card: the DOOR roster holds its pages at the strictest ceiling this tree writes, and most of them say nothing about it at their own door. Re-measured at this lap's head the roster reads 19 pages, 7 declaring Door and 12 silent -- two with no Style line at all and ten declaring a register while leaving the setting open. The gated question beside it, whether the meter should DERIVE its tiers from a page's declaration, stays Keaton's word; making a page state the ceiling it has already agreed to is a different act and needs no word.
think The sweep chose no new spelling. Measured across the living pages, the tree writes a declared setting two ways -- `Gauge, <Setting> setting` in 32 pages and `Gauge <Setting>` in 22 -- and the roster's own majority writes the first, so seven pages took one clause inserted into the line they already carried. README.md and the-first-hour.md declare Bhakta, which is a register rather than a dial, so both now read `Bhakta at the Door setting`: the register they are written in, and the setting Gauge reads them on.
think The twelfth page refused the sweep on the law's own grounds. foundations/20260823-034321_the-return-that-feeds-everyone.md carries a one-clock stamp in its basename, so it is testimony, and testimony keeps the words it wrote. The roster may hold a founding statement to the front-door share because a reading takes nothing away, while asking that page for a clause would be an edit to a dated artifact. So door_setting_verdict() answers testimony on a dated basename and counts it apart from the wall.
think The instrument reads the Style line through the one reader the card publishes rather than through a copy. prose_register_scan.sh lifts declared_style_line_of() and QA_HEAD_LINES out of tools/fixtures/q/qa_report_card.sh at run time with sed, the way qa_setting_declared_scan.sh already does, so three instruments read one line one way. A scan that cannot reach that reader refuses with verdict=reader_absent, since reading every door as silent and being unable to read at all look identical from outside and only one of them is a fault.
think The wall is drawn around the roster and stops there. The population census counts the same question across 330 pages and gates nothing, because a page at large may honestly name no setting; a page named in DOOR has already agreed to a ceiling, so the promise costs it one clause. The wall says a door declares its setting, and says nothing about the meter reading that declaration.
obs Handing the pens the card's absolute path was owed the moment the citation landed. Four existing legs went red on the first control run -- the census and law pens run the scan from their own directories, where the relative path to the card resolves nowhere -- and each was repaired by passing PROSE_CARD_READER rather than by softening the refusal. Two of those pens also needed their own rostered front door to declare Door, which is the wall biting its own control exactly as it bites the tree.
obs Both new halves were mutation-proven before the lap closed. Dropping door_undeclared from the verdict condition reads door_gate_bites=no while door_gate_lifts stays yes, so the two legs tell the gate from the reading. Dropping the testimony arm makes the scan demand a clause from a dated foundation and answer verdict=door_setting_undeclared -- the exemption is load-bearing rather than decorative.
obs The eleven swept pages all read B or better at Door with --service 90, so no frame was pushed: README.md A 94, bat/README.md A 96, the-first-hour A 94, mycelium B 80. Mycelium is the door nearest the edge in two readings at once -- 20% negative against a 20% ceiling, and reach 50 at grade 13 -- and its repair is a rewrite rather than a clause, so it is named here rather than started.
obs One wording change rode along and is named rather than hidden: the-first-hour's Style line ended "it stays in the field", which my last lap measured as one of two false positives the setting match cannot tell from a declaration. It now reads "it stays in the maintainer's working tree", which is what the sentence meant.
obs Shelving the elder block broke two of its relative links, and the guard caught it mid-hot-pass: a block written at the pin's depth carries `archive/...` and `../active-designing/...`, which mean different files one directory down. fold_shelf_link_repoint fixed both by computing the repair rather than guessing it. Applying that fix moved the tree under a running hot pass, so that pass was stopped by its pid -- verified as mine through /proc/<pid>/cwd, never by pattern -- and relaunched over the staged set, since a close that reads tree_moved=yes measures neither the tree it opened on nor the tree the commit ships.
